        <fieldset class="path-picker" id="pick-your-path">
            <legend class="visually-hidden">Where should Parachute run?</legend>

            <input class="path-radio" type="radio" name="install-path" id="path-local"
                   aria-controls="panel-local">
            <input class="path-radio" type="radio" name="install-path" id="path-do"
                   aria-controls="panel-do" checked>

            <div class="path-cards">
                <label class="path-card" for="path-local">
                    <span class="path-card-title">Your Mac/Linux device</span>
                    <span class="path-card-desc">Try it in a minute &mdash; runs on your own machine, sleeps when it sleeps. Free, nothing to rent.</span>
                </label>

                <label class="path-card path-card-recommended" for="path-do">
                    <span class="path-card-flag" aria-hidden="true">&#9733; Recommended</span>
                    <span class="path-card-title">DigitalOcean &mdash; always-on</span>
                    <span class="path-card-desc">A real Parachute your phone and your AIs can reach around the clock, even when your laptop's shut. Zero-SSH: open the Console, paste one line, watch it run. ~$6/mo.</span>
                </label>
            </div>

            <div class="path-panels">

                {# ---- PATH 1: Your Mac/Linux device ---- #}
                <div class="path-panel" id="panel-local" role="region" aria-labelledby="path-local">
                    <h3>Run it on your Mac or Linux device</h3>
                    <p>Perfect for kicking the tires. Free, runs in a minute. The catch: it sleeps when your machine sleeps, so your phone and your AIs can't reach it while it's closed. You'll need <a href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bun</a> 1.3 or later, then one command:</p>

                    <div class="code-inline">
                        <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code>
                    </div>

                    <p><code>parachute init</code> starts your hub, installs your vault &mdash; the place all your notes live &mdash; and opens a browser setup wizard: <strong>Account &rarr; Vault &rarr; Expose &rarr; Done</strong>. On a laptop the Expose step auto-skips; you're signed in locally.</p>

                    <details>
                        <summary>On Bun 1.2.x / checking your version</summary>
                        <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                            <li><strong>Check it.</strong> <code>bun --version</code> &mdash; if it's below 1.3, run <code>bun upgrade</code>.</li>
                            <li><strong>Stuck on 1.2.x?</strong> You may see a cosmetic <code>InvalidPackageResolution</code> or <code>FileNotFound</code> warning during install &mdash; the package installs anyway. Upgrading to Bun 1.3 clears it.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </details>

                    <details>
                        <summary>Ports, exposing it, tokens, and re-running</summary>
                        <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                            <li><strong>Ports.</strong> The hub answers on <code>1939</code>; the vault runs on <code>1940</code> behind it.</li>
                            <li><strong>Going beyond your laptop.</strong> <code>init</code> offers to expose your hub publicly (a Cloudflare Tunnel is the default on a server &mdash; it'll offer to install <code>cloudflared</code> for you on macOS and Linux). If an expose attempt fails, <code>init</code> keeps going on the local URL and prints a retry hint: <code>parachute expose public --cloudflare</code>.</li>
                            <li><strong>Bootstrap token.</strong> When the hub is exposed publicly and no admin exists yet, <code>init</code> prints a one-time bootstrap token; the wizard asks for it before it lets you create the first admin (local-only installs skip this).</li>
                            <li><strong>Safe to re-run.</strong> <code>init</code> is idempotent. Check the stack any time with <code>parachute status</code>.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </details>

                    <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">Want it reachable from your phone and your AIs around the clock? Move to an <a href="#pick-your-path" class="switch-to-do">always-on DigitalOcean box</a> anytime &mdash; same stack, nothing's locked in.</p>
                </div>

                {# ---- PATH 2: DigitalOcean (default) ---- #}
                <div class="path-panel" id="panel-do" role="region" aria-labelledby="path-do">
                    <h3>Stand up an always-on home on DigitalOcean</h3>
                    <p>The simplest way to a real always-on Parachute: create a droplet, open its <strong>Console</strong> in your browser, paste <strong>one line</strong>, and watch it run. When it finishes it prints your <strong>bootstrap token</strong> and <strong>URL</strong> right there &mdash; open the URL, set your username + password <strong>in the browser</strong>, done. <strong>No SSH, no separate token hunt.</strong> The box self-installs hub + vault and gets a real Let's Encrypt cert via <a href="https://caddyserver.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caddy</a>.</p>

                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Create an Ubuntu 24.04 droplet.</strong> In the DigitalOcean console (<a href="https://cloud.digitalocean.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cloud.digitalocean.com</a>) &rarr; <strong>Create &rarr; Droplets</strong>: image <strong>Ubuntu 24.04 (LTS) x64</strong>, size <strong>$6/mo (1 GB)</strong> for hub + vault (pick $12/mo / 2 GB for on-box transcription or to run Claude Code on the box), region nearest you. An SSH key is optional &mdash; you won't need it.</li>
                        <li><strong>Open the droplet's Console.</strong> On your droplet's page click <strong>Access &rarr; Launch Droplet Console</strong> &mdash; a root terminal right in your browser, no SSH needed. (If it prompts for a password, set one on that Access tab first.)</li>
                        <li><strong>Paste this one line and run it:</strong>
                            <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left; margin-top:0.6rem;">
<code>curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/digitalocean.sh | bash</code>
                            </div>
                        </li>
                        <li><strong>Wait ~2&ndash;3 minutes.</strong> When it finishes, it prints your <strong>bootstrap token</strong> (<code>parachute-bootstrap-&hellip;</code>) and your <strong>setup URL</strong> right there in the Console.</li>
                        <li><strong>Open that URL</strong> &mdash; it lands on <code>/admin/setup</code>. Paste the token, then choose your own username + password. That's it &mdash; you're the owner.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.9em;"><strong>Your password is set in the browser, never in the config.</strong> No username or password is ever typed into the cloud config &mdash; the hub mints a one-time bootstrap token on first boot, and you set your own password in the browser. Nothing sensitive persists in DigitalOcean. The token is single-use and regenerates if the hub restarts; re-read the current one from the Console any time with <code>cat parachute-setup.txt</code>.</p>

                    <details style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
                        <summary>No domain needed (and how to use your own)</summary>
                        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;"><strong>No domain needed.</strong> Run it as-is and you get a working HTTPS address at <code>https://&lt;your-droplet-ip&gt;.sslip.io</code> automatically. <a href="https://sslip.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sslip.io</a> is free wildcard DNS that resolves that name straight to your droplet, so the URL <em>and</em> the Let's Encrypt cert just work with zero DNS setup. Switch to a custom domain anytime.</p>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Using your own domain?</strong> First point it at the droplet &mdash; in your DNS provider add an <strong>A record</strong> for the hostname you want (e.g. <code>vault.example.com</code>) &rarr; your droplet's public IPv4 (shown in the DO dashboard). Then run it with the domain set:</p>
                        <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left;">
<code>PARACHUTE_DOMAIN=vault.example.com curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/digitalocean.sh | bash</code>
                        </div>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">(or <code>export PARACHUTE_DOMAIN=&hellip;</code> first). Caddy fetches a Let's Encrypt cert for it automatically.</p>
                    </details>

                    <details style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
                        <summary>Prefer unattended / Infrastructure-as-Code?</summary>
                        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Paste the <em>same</em> one-liner into the droplet's <strong>User Data</strong> when you create it (Advanced options &rarr; Add Initialization scripts), prefixed with a <code>#!/bin/bash</code> line &mdash; it self-installs on first boot, no Console step:</p>
                        <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left;">
<code>#!/bin/bash<br>
curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/digitalocean.sh | bash</code>
                        </div>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">Then grab your bootstrap token once the box is up &mdash; over SSH:</p>
                        <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left;">
<code>ssh root@&lt;your-droplet-ip&gt; cat parachute-setup.txt</code>
                        </div>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">(or open the Console and <code>cat parachute-setup.txt</code>). For a domain in this path, add <code>PARACHUTE_DOMAIN=vault.example.com</code> on its own <code>export</code> line above the <code>curl</code>.</p>
                    </details>

                    <details style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
                        <summary>Change your domain later</summary>
                        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Already running and want a different address (e.g. moving off the <code>sslip.io</code> name to your own domain)? First point the new domain at the box &mdash; in your DNS provider add an <strong>A record</strong> for the hostname (e.g. <code>vault.example.com</code>) &rarr; your droplet's public IPv4. Then, on the box, run one command:</p>
                        <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left;">
<code>parachute hub set-origin https://vault.example.com</code>
                        </div>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">It rewrites Caddy to the new hostname, fetches a fresh Let's Encrypt cert, and restarts your modules &mdash; one command. Your data, account, and vault are untouched; only the address changes. Afterward, <strong>re-point your AI / MCP clients</strong> at the new URL (their tokens were minted for the old address).</p>
                    </details>
                </div>

            </div>
        </fieldset>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>2 &middot; Finish the wizard</h2>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Account</span> <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);">&rarr;</span>
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Vault</span> <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);">&rarr;</span>
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Expose</span> <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);">&rarr;</span>
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Done</span>
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Account.</strong> Create your admin login (on DigitalOcean this is the browser <code>/admin/setup</code> step above &mdash; paste the bootstrap token, choose your username + password).</li>
            <li><strong>Vault.</strong> Create your first vault &mdash; or import one from a git repo, or skip for now. A fresh vault opens with a <strong>Getting Started</strong> note: a short guide your AI reads to help you shape the vault around how you actually think and work.</li>
            <li><strong>Expose.</strong> Choose how (or whether) to reach your hub from outside. This step <em>auto-skips on a local laptop</em>, and <em>pre-selects public on an SSH'd server</em> so the wizard URL is reachable.</li>
            <li><strong>Done.</strong> The wizard hands you everything you need to connect your AI.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="connect-mcp-clients">
        <h2>3 &middot; Connect your AI</h2>

        <p>Connecting any AI &mdash; Claude.ai on the web, ChatGPT, Claude Code, Cursor, Zed, your own agent &mdash; comes down to one URL: your vault's MCP endpoint. Point your client at it (web UIs call this a <strong>connector</strong>):</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code>
        </div>

        <p><code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;</code> is the address your hub answers on &mdash; the same URL you sign in at, <em>not</em> the vault's own port <code>1940</code>. <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> is your vault name (<code>default</code> unless you changed it). The <strong><code>/mcp</code> suffix is required</strong> &mdash; the bare path is a metadata page, not the endpoint. OAuth-capable clients send you through hub sign-in automatically.</p>

        <p>Pick your client:</p>

        <details open>
            <summary>Claude.ai, ChatGPT &amp; other web AIs</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">In your AI's settings, add a new <strong>connector</strong> (custom MCP server) pointing at <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code>. You'll be sent to your hub to sign in and approve &mdash; then the AI can read and write your vault. Nothing to install; the easiest path for most people.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Claude Code &amp; other CLI / MCP clients</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Register the server once with <code>claude mcp add</code> &mdash; the wizard's done screen gives you a ready-to-paste command. The first time it connects, it sends you through your hub's sign-in in the browser (OAuth); after that, start a new session (MCP config loads at session start) and run <code>/mcp</code> &mdash; the vault's tools are there. Codex, Goose, Cursor, and Zed take the same <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code> URL. (Headless box that can't do the browser flow? Mint a token on the vault's Tokens page and append <code>--header "Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;"</code>.)</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Clients that need a bearer token</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Some clients can't do OAuth and want a static token instead. Mint one from the vault admin SPA's <strong>Tokens</strong> page, then give it to the client alongside the same <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code> URL.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>&#9733; 4 &middot; See your first note</h2>

        <p>Here's the payoff. Ask your AI to jot a quick note (&ldquo;jot down that I'm trying Parachute today&rdquo;), then open it in a browser:</p>

        <p><strong>Want a quick look right now? Nothing to install.</strong> Open <a href="https://notes.parachute.computer" target="_blank">notes.parachute.computer</a>, the hosted Notes app. Enter your hub's vault URL (<code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;</code>) and sign in. The app runs in your browser; your notes never leave your hub. There's the note you just made &mdash; a zero-install peek while you build a surface of your own (below).</p>

        <div class="starter-callout">
            <h3>Fill your vault &mdash; let your AI walk you through it</h3>
            <p>Your vault starts mostly empty &mdash; but it opens with a <strong>Getting Started</strong> note your AI reads to do the rest. Just ask your connected AI something like <em>&ldquo;read the Getting Started note and help me set up my parachute.&rdquo;</em> It interviews you about how you think, proposes a tag and folder structure, and imports what you already have &mdash; all on your say-so. The same note points your AI at building a surface when you're ready.</p>
            <p class="starter-adjacent">(Power users: you can also <a href="/scripting/">script your vault</a> from bash, Python, or JS for imports, cron jobs, and CI.)</p>
        </div>
    </section>

