    <section class="section">
        <h2>Make it yours &mdash; build a custom surface</h2>

        <p>Everything lives in an open vault your AI can read and write, so you build a UI shaped to <em>your</em> brain &mdash; a dashboard, a daily journal, a garden &mdash; in your own codebase, no Parachute install needed. <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@openparachute/surface-client" target="_blank"><code>@openparachute/surface-client</code></a> gives you OAuth sign-in + the vault API, and <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@openparachute/surface-render" target="_blank"><code>surface-render</code></a> renders your notes &mdash; a working surface in a few lines. It's a static SPA, so you <strong>host it free on GitHub Pages</strong> (or any static host); it talks straight to your hub over HTTPS.</p>

        <figure style="margin: 0;">
            <div class="surface-shot-frame">
                <img src="/assets/install/unisurface.png" alt="A custom Parachute surface over a personal vault, with the quick-capture panel open and the background dimmed behind it" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1000">
            </div>
            <figcaption class="surface-shot-cap">My personal surface &mdash; &ldquo;a quiet garden for the mind&rdquo;: a custom surface built on top of my own vault.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Your vault's <strong>Getting Started</strong> note (and the <strong>Surface Starter</strong> note it links to) walks your AI through this end to end &mdash; scaffolding the SPA on the SDK and deploying it to GitHub Pages. Open it in Claude Code or your editor, give it your hub URL and a description of what you look at most, and build.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Going further <span style="font-weight:400;color:var(--fg-dim);">(optional)</span></h2>

        <p>The four steps above are the whole first run. Everything here is for later.</p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Still on your laptop?</strong> Move to an always-on home anytime &mdash; the <a href="#pick-your-path" class="switch-to-do">DigitalOcean path above</a> is the simplest (one command). Any other always-on box works the same way: a Hetzner or EC2 VPS, a machine in your closet, or a Mac you leave on, all run the same <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code> flow. On an SSH'd server <code>init</code> pre-selects public exposure so the wizard URL is reachable.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Reach it from other devices.</strong> The DigitalOcean path above sets up Caddy-direct for you &mdash; a real Let's Encrypt cert on your own HTTPS address, no tunnel. On any other box, <code>parachute expose public</code> gives you a public URL: add <code>--cloudflare --domain vault.example.com</code> for a <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/products/tunnel/" target="_blank">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> on your own domain (the apex must already be a Cloudflare zone &mdash; the free tier is fine; <code>expose</code> <em>creates</em> the tunnel for you, so don't pre-create it, and each machine gets its own per-hostname tunnel plus a reboot-surviving connector). Drop <code>--cloudflare</code> for a domain-free <code>*.ts.net</code> public URL over <a href="https://tailscale.com" target="_blank">Tailscale</a> Funnel; or <code>tailscale serve</code> keeps the hub private to your own tailnet.</li>
            <li><strong>Back it up &amp; script it.</strong> Export your whole vault to portable, git-trackable markdown with <code>parachute-vault export &lt;dir&gt;</code> (and <code>import</code> back). Driving the vault from a script, cron job, or importer is covered in <a href="/scripting/">Scripting your vault</a>; hosting for friends and family is in the <a href="/multi-user/">multi-user operator guide</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Stay current.</strong> <code>parachute upgrade</code> sweeps the hub and every installed service to the latest release. Add <code>--channel rc</code> to track release candidates. Vault migrations run automatically on first post-upgrade boot.</li>
        </ul>

        <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;"><strong>Want Parachute Cloud?</strong> A hosted version &mdash; no server to run &mdash; is on the roadmap. <a href="https://openparachute.substack.com" target="_blank">Subscribe for updates</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

        <details>
            <summary>Something not working?</summary>
            <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                <li><strong>Claude Code doesn't see the vault.</strong> Start a new session (MCP config loads at session start) and run <code>/mcp</code>. Still missing? Re-run <code>parachute-vault mcp-install</code> on the hub machine.</li>
                <li><strong>Port 1940 already in use.</strong> Run <code>lsof -i :1940</code> to see what's there; change ports in <code>~/.parachute/vault/.env</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>A client can't reach the MCP endpoint.</strong> Confirm the URL ends in <code>/mcp</code> and points at the <em>hub</em> origin (the address you sign in at), not the vault's own port <code>1940</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Stuck on Bun 1.2.x?</strong> You may see a cosmetic <code>InvalidPackageResolution</code> or <code>FileNotFound</code> warning &mdash; the package installs anyway. Upgrading to Bun 1.3 clears it.</li>
                <li>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Claude connector fails right after consent over a Cloudflare tunnel</summary>
                        <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">You exposed your hub publicly with a <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/products/tunnel/" target="_blank">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> (<code>parachute expose public --cloudflare</code>) and tried to add your vault to the <strong>Claude Desktop / claude.ai / mobile MCP connector</strong>. OAuth consent succeeds, then Claude reports <em>&ldquo;the integration rejected the credentials&rdquo;</em> or <em>&ldquo;the connection was reverted&rdquo;</em> and never reaches the vault &mdash; while Notes and Claude Code (which use a static token) keep working fine, so it looks like an OAuth bug.</p>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Cause &mdash; this is a Cloudflare edge setting, not a Parachute bug.</strong> Consent runs in your <em>browser</em>, so it passes Cloudflare. But the token exchange (<code>POST /oauth/token</code>) and the MCP calls are <em>server-to-server</em> requests from Anthropic's servers &mdash; and Cloudflare's <strong>Bot Fight Mode</strong> / <strong>Super Bot Fight Mode</strong> / <strong>Browser Integrity Check</strong> (or a Cloudflare Access policy) challenges them with a <code>403</code> &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; page. Claude gets the 403 instead of a token.</p>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Fix &mdash; in your Cloudflare dashboard for the exposed zone:</strong></p>
                        <ul style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                            <li>Turn <strong>off</strong> Bot Fight Mode / Super Bot Fight Mode (Security &rarr; Bots), <em>or</em> add a <strong>WAF Skip rule</strong> scoped to your exposed hostname (or the API paths <code>/oauth/*</code>, <code>/.well-known/*</code>, <code>/vault/*</code>) that skips Bot Fight Mode, Managed Rules, and the Browser Integrity Check.</li>
                            <li>If <strong>Browser Integrity Check</strong> is on, disable it (or skip it for those paths).</li>
                            <li>If you put the hostname behind <strong>Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust)</strong>, remove that policy &mdash; the hub does its own OAuth, and an outer Access gate blocks the API.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Confirm it's this.</strong> From any non-browser tool, hit your public origin with a non-browser user-agent &mdash; e.g. <code>curl -A python-requests https://&lt;your-host&gt;/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server</code>. If Cloudflare's <code>403</code> &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; HTML page comes back (rather than a hub response) while a normal browser loads the same URL fine, that's the cause. The failed token exchange also won't appear in <code>~/.parachute/hub/logs/hub.log</code> &mdash; Cloudflare blocked it before it ever reached the hub.</p>
                    </details>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Found something we missed? <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/issues" target="_blank">Open an issue</a>.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

    <section class="cta">
        <div class="code-inline" style="margin: 1.5rem auto 0.75rem; max-width: 640px;">
            <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code>
        </div>
